%== What is interesting about the upper bounds
Previous work by Bannach et al.~\cite{BannachCT23}
showed that in the weighted definability setting, formulas
with the pattern~$ae$ already suffice to describe $\Class{W}[2]$-hard
problems. We now show that the situation is more favorable in the
vertex deletion setting, which is a special case of weighted
definability: All problems in $\PVD{basic}(e^*a^*e^*)$ are tractable
and the problems in $\PVD{basic}(e^*a^*)$ and in $\PVD{basic}(eae)$ 
are even in $\Para\Class{AC}^0$, the smallest class commonly
considered in parameterized complexity. We start with the last claim:

\begin{lemma~}\label{lemma:vd-basic-eae}
directed graphs all have provably distinct complexity characterizations.

