    copies $u_i$ of~$u$. The size-$1$ set cover $C = \{s_2\}$
    corresponds to the fact that deleting exactly $s_2$ (or exactly
    $s_2'$) from the right graph yields a graph in which each vertex
    has an incident edge that is not part of a triangle. The same is
    true for the size-$2$ set cover $C = \{s_1,s_3\}$. In contrast,
    $C = \{s_1\}$ is not a set cover as $v$ is not covered and,
    indeed, all four incident edges of~$v_1$ (namely $s_2 \adj' v_1$,
    $s_2' \adj' v_1$, $s_3\adj' v_1$, and $s_3'\adj' v_1$) are part of
    triangles, if we  delete none of $s_2$, $s_s'$, $s_3$, or~$s_3'$.
