1914-situation as in that lemma, when <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is interpreted as
1915:<math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}(s,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}(s,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course, <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> also talks about adjacency
1916-to <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> in the form of atoms <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\adj x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\adj x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\adj y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\adj y</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
1917-also talks about self-loops in the form of atoms <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\adj x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\adj x</annotation></semantics></math> and
1918-<math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\adj y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\adj y</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it turns out that these are in one-to-one
--
1926-<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
1927-<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In a bit more detail, let us go over the proof of
1928-Lemma <a href="#S3.SS0.SSS0.P0.SPx1" title="Upper Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> once more. We first defined that a
1929:vertex <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">witness for some <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V\setminus\{s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V\setminus\{s\}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math></em> if <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}(s,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}(s,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> held. Our new definition
1930-is now simply that <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is a witness for <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> if
1931-<math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> holds – the vertex <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> no longer used or needed, just
1932-like the notion of something begin “relative to <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>”. Next, we
--
2030-</figcaption>
2031-</figure>
2032-<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
2033:<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The idea for this proof is the same as in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ Lower Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, but instead of
2034-encoding three colors with two special vertices, we use one special vertex and
2035-self-loops.</p>
2036-</div>
2037-<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
2038-<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The formula.</em> Define the formula
2039:<math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-undirected-eae}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S4" title="4 Undirected Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-undirected-eae}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: Take the formula
2040:<math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-basic-eeae}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ Lower Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-basic-eeae}}</annotation></semantics></math> from
2041:page <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ Lower Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, but remove the quantifier
2042-<math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (which yields the desired pattern <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="eae" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">eae</annotation></semantics></math>) and replace
2043-each occurrence of <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\adj c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\adj c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\adj v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\adj v</annotation></semantics></math> and occurrence of <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{false}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑓𝑎𝑙𝑠𝑒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{false}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is any variable.</p>
2044-</div>
2045-<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
2046-<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The reduction.</em> We only describe the difference to the
2047:reduction from Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ Lower Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>: We do not add
2048-<math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, for each vertex <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for which we used to have
2049-<math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\adj^{\prime}u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\adj^{\prime}u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we add a self-loop instead, so <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\adj^{\prime}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">∼</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\adj^{\prime}v</annotation></semantics></math> holds
2050-instead.</p>
--
2052-<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
2053-<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Correctness.</em> In our construction of both the formula and of
2054-the graph, “<math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>” got replaced by “<math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> has a
2055:self-loop” and, thus, the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 11. ‣ Lower Bounds ‣ 3 Basic Graphs ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>
2056-can be recycled. It only remains to argue that it is not possible
2057-that deleting <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> would have produced solutions that are no longer
2058-possible, but reviewing the proof shows that we already argued there
--
