2159-</div>
2160-<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
2161:<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Forward direction.</em> We have that <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,s,t)\in\mathrm{p}\text{{-matched-reach}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">-matched-reach</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,s,t)\in\mathrm{p}\text{{-matched-reach}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and show that we have <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\prime},k^{\prime})\in\mathrm{p\text{-}}\mathchoice{\text{\small$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{\small$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{$\mathrm{VD}$}}_{\mathrm{dir}}(\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-directed-ae}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mi mathsize="0.900em">VD</mi></mrow><mi>dir</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S5" title="5 Directed Graphs and Arbitrary Structures ‣ On the Descriptive Complexity of Vertex Deletion Problems" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime},k^{\prime})\in\mathrm{p\text{-}}\mathchoice{\text{\small$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{\small$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{$\mathrm{VD}$}}{\text{$\mathrm{VD}$}}_{\mathrm{dir}}(\phi_{\ref{lemma:vd-directed-ae}})</annotation></semantics></math>. To make the
2162-formula true, we simply delete every vertex in the original path of <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and
2163-since <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is in the same original path, we have to delete exactly <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
2164-vertices. Now every vertex has a successor: The vertices in the layers <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
--
2166-in <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has itself as a successor via the self-loop.</p>
2167-</div>
